  "explanation": {
    "summary": "To turn a statement into a yes/no question in Japanese, you don't change the word order at all. You just add the small particle か (ka) to the very end. So 今日は あめです (Today is rain) becomes 今日は あめですか (Is today rain? = Is it rainy today?). か works like a spoken question mark, so a full Japanese question still ends with 。 and not ？. When you answer, you can start with はい (yes) or いいえ (no) and often drop the topic, as in いいえ、くもりです (No, it's cloudy).",
    "structure_notes": [
      "Slot order: [Topic] は + [Noun] + です + か. The new part is only the last slot, か.",
      "Unlike English, nothing moves. English swaps 'it is' to 'is it', but Japanese keeps the order and just adds か at the end.",
      "か is a particle that marks the whole sentence as a question. Your voice usually rises a little on か.",
      "In answers, the topic is often left out because everyone already knows it: (今日は) くもりです → くもりです.",
      "Weather words like あめ (rain), はれ (sunny) and くもり (cloudy) are nouns here, so they fit straight into the [Noun] slot before です."
    ],
    "connects_to": "This is the X は Y です pattern from episode 1 with one extra particle, か, added at the end. Any statement you can already make, you can now ask as a question."
  },
